## §4.4 H₂ Hamiltonian (STO-3G, 4-qubit form) — Eq. after Eq. (16)
$$
H_{\mathrm{H_2}} = f_0 \; \mathbb{1} \otimes \mathbb{1}
                 + f_1 \; \sigma^z \otimes \sigma^z
                 + f_2 \; \sigma^z \otimes \mathbb{1}
                 + f_3 \; \mathbb{1} \otimes \sigma^z
                 + f_4 \; \sigma^x \otimes \sigma^x
$$

The coefficients $f_i$ depend on the H–H distance $R$. Diagonalization gives the
ground-state energy $E_0(R)$; the dissociation curve is shown in Fig. 4a.

## §5.1 QAOA cost + mixer Hamiltonians — Eqs. (17)–(18)

Cost Hamiltonian (diagonal in $Z$-basis, matches classical optimization problem):
$$
H_C \;=\; \sum_\alpha h_\alpha P_\alpha \;=\; \sum_\alpha h_\alpha \bigotimes_{i_\alpha} \sigma^z_{i_\alpha} \qquad (17)
$$

Mixer Hamiltonian (global transverse field):
$$
H_M \;=\; -\sum_i \sigma^x_i
$$

QAOA evolution operator of depth $D$:
$$
U(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\gamma}) \;=\; \prod_{l=1}^{D} e^{-i \beta_l H_M} \, e^{-i \gamma_l H_C} \qquad (18)
$$

acting on the uniform superposition $|+\rangle^{\otimes N}$. The variational parameters
$(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\gamma})$ are optimized to minimize
$\langle \psi(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\gamma})|H_C|\psi(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\gamma})\rangle$.

## §5.1 (verbatim) — 3-regular MaxCut approximation bound

The paper cites Farhi-Goldstone-Gutmann 2014 (refs [41,42]): for QAOA at depth
$p=1$ (i.e. $D=1$ in the paper's notation) on 3-regular graphs $G$, the
expected cut satisfies
$$
\langle C \rangle_{\mathrm{QAOA},p=1} \;\ge\; 0.6924 \cdot \mathrm{MaxCut}(G).
$$
This is the concrete testable claim extracted for the QAOA replication.

## §5.2 MaxCut cost function (weighted)
$$
C(z) \;=\; \tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{(i,j)\in E} w_{ij}\,(1 - z_i z_j), \qquad z_i \in \{-1,+1\}
$$
Unweighted MaxCut sets all $w_{ij}=1$; on a 3-regular graph the number of edges
is $|E| = 3n/2$.

## §4.4 (paraphrase) — chemical accuracy claim
"For hardware-efficient trial states, entangler depths $D \approx 8$ suffice for
$\mathrm{H_2}$ within chemical accuracy (≈1 mHa), while $D=28$ is required for
LiH and BeH₂." — motivating the VQE-H₂ within chemical accuracy testable number
of $\pm 1.6$ mHa vs FCI = $-1.1373$ Ha at $R = 0.735$ Å.

